14. Non-Tariff Barriers and Import Quota: Quota versus Tariff
Paper: International Economics (ECNCC301) | Unit: III | Topic: Non-tariff barriers, Effects of import quota, Quota versus tariff for a large and small nation
Introduction
As tariffs have fallen through GATT/WTO rounds, governments increasingly use non-tariff barriers (NTBs) to restrict trade. Their effects are often harder to measure and more distorting.
Types of NTBs
• Quantitative restrictions: import quotas, voluntary export restraints (VERs), licensing.
• Technical barriers: standards, labelling, sanitary and phytosanitary rules.
• Customs procedures: valuation, delays, rules of origin.
• Subsidies and government procurement preferences.
• Anti-dumping and countervailing duties.
• Local content requirements and foreign exchange controls.
Effects of an import quota
A quota limits imports to a fixed quantity. Under perfect competition in a small country, domestic price rises until import demand equals the quota, just as with an equivalent tariff. Consumption falls, domestic production rises and there is a deadweight loss. The difference from a tariff is who gets the quota rents (the price gap times quota quantity). They go to:
• The government, if it auctions licences (equivalent to a tariff).
• Domestic importers, if licences are given free.
• Foreign exporters, under a VER.
Quota versus tariff
1. Revenue: a tariff gives revenue to the government; a quota yields rents to licence holders unless auctioned.
2. Certainty: a quota fixes the import quantity; a tariff fixes the price but not the quantity.
3. Demand shifts: under a tariff, rising demand increases imports; under a quota, it raises price only.
4. Monopoly: a quota gives a domestic monopolist more market power, since competition from imports is capped. A tariff keeps the world price as a ceiling.
5. Corruption: licence allocation invites rent-seeking.
6. Transparency: tariffs are easier to negotiate and bind in the WTO.
Large versus small nation
For a small nation, an equivalent quota and tariff have the same effect on prices and welfare (apart from rents). For a large nation, both can improve terms of trade, but the quota rents may be captured by foreign exporters (under a VER), turning a gain into a loss. A tariff keeps revenue at home.
Conclusion
Quotas are generally less efficient and less transparent than tariffs, which is why the WTO requires conversion of NTBs into tariffs (tariffication).